</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table S.1: </span>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As seen in Figure <a href="#A2.F3" title="Figure S.3 ‣ Appendix B Silent Chain-of-Thought ‣ Emergent Manifold Separability during Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S.3</span></a>, we see that the manifold capacity of the child node increases during the parent’s computation even when the truth values of the child nodes are silenced during the CoT.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A2.F3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="A2.F3.2.1" class="ltx_text"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20338v1/normal_vs_silent.svg" id="A2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/169;" width="429" height="169"></object></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure S.3: </span>
<span id="A2.F3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison of Capacity dynamics between normal and silenced CoT.</span> (Left) Data is aligned to the moment a node is computed. There is no statistically significant difference between the two prompts, which is expected as the only difference in the prompts is in the reference to the children.
(Right) Data is aligned to the moment a node is recalled by its parent.
There is no significant difference between the two prompts, which confirms the linear separability of the children is functionally necessary to compute the parent’s value and does not merely reflect that the children are being recalled.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Taking a step further, we prompted Ministral to completely silence everything within the line of “*Logic:” structural token
(Table <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table S.2 ‣ Appendix B Silent Chain-of-Thought ‣ Emergent Manifold Separability during Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S.2</span></a>), masking the logic expression, e.g. “ ‘[05] and [06]’ ”, with “ ‘***************’ ”, which has an equal number of tokens. This way, we completely eliminate the possibility of capacity increasing simply due to textual reasons.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A2.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
